!   Wes Bordon's cyclobutanetetraone, (CO)4 ... 440 AOs, 368 MOs
!   The curiosity here is that singlet RHF is lower than triplet ROHF,
!   but dynamical correlation reverses this order, to a triplet G.S.
!   test case prepared in 2012
!
!   The test case is left as a ZAPT MP2 energy calculation.
!      FINAL ROHF E= -450.9300388936, 11 iters, E(MP2)= -452.5330850988
!   Various other results are below the inputs.
!
!   This run takes 983 seconds on 2.66 GHz Cloverton (31 Mar 2015)
!
!      theory:
!   X.Zhou, D.A.Hrovat, W.T.Bordon  J.Phys.Chem.A 114, 1304-1308(2010)
!      experiment:
!   J.-C.Guo, G.L.Hou, S.-D.Li, X.-B.Wang  J.Phys.Chem.Lett 3, 304-308(2012)
!      both agree this molecule should be 3-B1u, not 1-A1g.
!
!   Note atom order changes between D4h and D2h, so generate $VECs in
!   the chosen group.  For CI purposes, the Abelian D2h is more better.
!   The b1g and a2u orbitals of D4h are the ag and b1u irreps of D2h.
!
 $contrl runtyp=energy ispher=1 $end
 $basis  gbasis=acct $end
 $guess  guess=moread norb=368 $end
 $statpt opttol=1d-5 $end

!   GVB run from triplet starting orbs printed orb 29 has zero norm?
x$contrl scftyp=gvb mult=1 $end
x$scf    nco=27 npair=1 $end
!   ...so I just used the next inputs to perform the TCSCF run.
x$contrl scftyp=mcscf mult=1 $end
x$det    stsym=ag ncore=27 nact=2 nels=2 $end
x$trans  ipurfy=2 $end

!   special input to get orbitals to test for multireference character...
x$contrl scftyp=rhf mult=1 $end
x$scf    vvos=.true. $end
!   ...which is done by CI-SD in VVOs.
!   singlet= 10,793 determinants, triplet= 6,584 determinants
x$contrl scftyp=none cityp=ormas mult=1 $end
x$cidet  stsym=ag ncore=8 nact=32 nels=40 $end
x$contrl scftyp=none cityp=ormas mult=3 $end
x$cidet  stsym=b1u ncore=8 nact=32 nels=40 $end
x$ormas  nspace=2 mstart(1)=9,29 mine(1)=38,0 maxe(1)=40,2 $end

!   how to do MP2:   perhaps pick the optimized geometries for each!
x$contrl scftyp=rhf  mult=1 mplevl=2 $end
x$system parall=.true. mwords=10 memddi=150 $end
 $contrl scftyp=rohf mult=3 mplevl=2 $end
 $system parall=.true. mwords=10 memddi=150 $end
x$contrl scftyp=uhf  mult=3 mplevl=2 $end
x$system parall=.true. mwords=20 memddi=500 $end
 $scf    dirscf=.true. $end
 $mp2    code=ddi ospt=zapt $end

 $data
Wes Borden's (CO)4 ring...really it is D4h symmetry, with X=Y exactly
Dnh 2

C  6.0   0.7724609153   0.7724608585   0.0000000000
O  8.0   1.6223085307   1.6223082882   0.0000000000
 $end

                    detailed results:

The RHF configuration is (b1g)2(a2u)0 which is 8 pi electrons.
RHF/ACCT is E= -450.9817825588, eps(b1g)= -0.3902 and eps(a2u)= -0.0684
C  6.0   0.7784295094   0.7784295094   0.0000000000
O  8.0   1.6015910790   1.6015910790   0.0000000000

The ROHF configuration is (b1g)1(a2u)1 which is 9 pi electrons.
The full ROHF valence configuration in D4h is
   (a1g)2(eu)4(b2g)2(a1g)2(eu)4(b2g)2(a1g)2(b1g)2(eu)4
   (a2u)2(eg)4(b1u)2(a2g)2(eu)4(b1g)1(a2u)1
irreps with odd sigma-h character (pi orbs) are eg, a1u, a2u, b1u, b2u.
ROHF/ACCT is E= -450.9495601269, this and previous RHF done in D4h symmetry
C  6.0   0.7837211393   0.7837211393   0.0000000000
O  8.0   1.5976883100   1.5976883100   0.0000000000
If you care to do the triplet with UHF (D2h optimization):
C  6.0   0.7784121824   0.7784119161   0.0000000000  E= -450.9709645705
O  8.0   1.5970145768   1.5970170906   0.0000000000  with <S**2>= 2.175

A higher energy RHF configuration is (b1g)0(a2u)2 which is 10 pi electrons.
RHF/ACCT is E= -450.8896203698, so this is clearly out of the picture.
C  6.0   0.7869360874   0.7869360874   0.0000000000
O  8.0   1.5949210659   1.5949210659   0.0000000000

Instead it is more better to include both 1-A1g determinants in a TCSCF!
TCSCF/ACCT is E= -450.9821060459, occ.no.(b1g)= 1.9961, occ.no.(a2u)= 0.0039,
C  6.0   0.7784630315   0.7784629970   0.0000000000
O  8.0   1.6015780949   1.6015784102   0.0000000000
which doesn't seem very multi-reference.  Note D2h run relaxes the square.

Double checking, at RHF geom using RHF + VVOs, for both spin states:
1. valence CI-SD on the singlet state is E= -451.3312816629, occupancies:
  2.0000000000    2.0000000000    2.0000000000    2.0000000000    2.0000000000
  2.0000000000    2.0000000000    2.0000000000    1.9985860079    1.9985860079
  1.9984898684    1.9984465189    1.9946306328    1.9935736476    1.9935736476
  1.9931256036    1.9918890908    1.9897527298    1.9897527298    1.9889664742
  1.9874802141    1.9829697401    1.9829697400   *1.9814910010    1.9689262584
  1.9634063140    1.9634063140    1.9590118397   *0.0553095019    0.0365020295
  0.0365020295    0.0251428034    0.0212847908    0.0212847908    0.0211948704
  0.0174691705    0.0139167513    0.0117053169    0.0117053169    0.0089482471
     The two orbitals marked * are the b1g and a2u (D4h labeling) of the TCSCF.
2. valence CI-SD on the triplet state is E= -451.0244266441, occupancies:
  2.0000000000    2.0000000000    2.0000000000    2.0000000000    2.0000000000
  2.0000000000    2.0000000000    2.0000000000    1.9996877206    1.9996877206
  1.9996544636    1.9996150127    1.9992693948    1.9986343850    1.9986343850
  1.9983044720    1.9969737491    1.9962963731    1.9925257820    1.9925257820
  1.9891228632    1.9880779698    1.9880779698    1.9847244732    1.9827971352
  1.9687070872    1.9687070872   *1.0782392462   *0.9543715385    0.0371845302
  0.0371845302    0.0136251513    0.0093450071    0.0077916277    0.0077916277
  0.0042007855    0.0032575844    0.0018806868    0.0018806868    0.0012231712
     The two orbitals marked * are the b1g and a2u (D4h labeling) of the ROHF.
     Ignore the energy comparison, both runs used the RHF geometry.
Nothing here to make you think single ref. many body methods aren't OK.
Note that the number of determinants in the CI-SD is not heavy, so one
could do the ORMAS-type MCSCF if need be.

The paper indicates RMP2 and UMP2 give the triplet higher than the singlet,
but that CCSD(T) and various kinds of DFT predict a lower triplet.
MP2 is fast to try, and we can geometry optimize too:
   1-A1g   RHF= -450.981782   RMP2//RHF = -452.493377  RMP2= -452.503760
   3-B2u  ROHF= -450.949560   ZAPT//ROHF= -452.516948  ZAPT= -452.533085
   3-B2u   UHF= -450.970965   UMP2//UHF = -452.501667  UMP2= -452.506599
Here the prediction of a lower triplet shows up already at the MP2 level.
Note that I am not using Bordon's basis set.

Geometry for RMP2:   (MP2s run in D2h, so lose perfect squareness)
   C  6.0   0.7812740370   0.7812740230   0.0000000000
   O  8.0   1.6321536762   1.6321538378   0.0000000000
Geometry for ZAPT:
   C  6.0   0.7724609153   0.7724608585   0.0000000000
   O  8.0   1.6223085307   1.6223082882   0.0000000000
Geometry for UMP2:   (S**2= 2.216 here)
   C  6.0   0.7824034377   0.7824036560   0.0000000000
   O  8.0   1.6179396934   1.6179380551   0.0000000000


=========================================================================
The orbitals are prepared in D2h.  These can produce the right triplet
state, as the two orbitals at HOMO/LUMO boundary are the right ones.
And they will converge at any geometry you please.

